  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nModel ensembles show the broad upper level low maintaining its \ninfluence over the Pacific Northwest through the weekend before \ngradually shifting eastward early next week. On Saturday, \npersistent southwesterly flow aloft will maintain a chance of \nshowers, primarily focused over the higher terrain of central \nIdaho and eastern Oregon, while lower valleys experience lower \nprecipitation odds. The upper level low pushes east across the \narea on Sunday and this is when it could potentially link up \nwith moisture from Hurricane Marie, increasing the threat for \nheavier precipitation across south-central Idaho. Overall \nconfidence remains low at this time, but a growing number of \nensembles have shifted towards this moisture moving across the \nGreat Basin in the latest runs. \n\nAs the main low axis finally crosses the Northern Rockies\nMonday, cooler temperatures and additional shower activity will\nsweep through the region. Snow levels may lower enough to bring\nlight snow or mixed precipitation to the highest peaks. By\nTuesday and Wednesday, ensemble clusters favor a transition to\ndrier conditions and a subtle warming trend as an upper level\nridge attempts to build inland from the Pacific coast. High\ntemperatures throughout the long term period will remain\ngenerally near or slightly below seasonal averages.",
